{"metadata":{"role":"article","title":"Architecture & Performance","roleHeading":"Article","modules":[{"name":"Swidux"}]},"abstract":[{"text":"Understand the architectural patterns that make Swidux work correctly with SwiftUI’s observation system.","type":"text"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","level":2,"anchor":"Overview","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Swidux relies on specific patterns to ensure correct "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Observable"},{"type":"text","text":" behavior, efficient rendering, and safe concurrency. This article explains the reasoning behind these patterns."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"text":"The Snapshot Pattern","level":2,"anchor":"The-Snapshot-Pattern","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store.send()"},{"type":"text","text":" copies the observer tree into a local struct, mutates the copy via the reducer, then assigns changed properties back. This happens internally via "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"SwiduxObservable"},{"type":"text","text":" — "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"State(observer:)"},{"type":"text","text":" packs, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"State.apply(_:to:)"},{"type":"text","text":" unpacks."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The pattern exists for two reasons:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"orderedList","items":[{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" equality checking."}]},{"type":"text","text":" The "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"set"},{"type":"text","text":" accessor checks "},{"code":"Equatable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and suppresses no-op notifications. Swift’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"_modify","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" accessor (used by ","type":"text"},{"code":"inout","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") fires notifications unconditionally. The snapshot pattern routes through ","type":"text"},{"code":"set","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Cross-slice observation isolation."}]},{"text":" The ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Swidux"},{"text":" macro generates a separate ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Observable"},{"text":" class for each ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Slice"},{"text":" property. A view reading ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"store.items"},{"text":" won’t re-render when only ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"store.ui"},{"text":" changes, because they live on different observer objects.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"code":"@Swidux","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" macro generates the observer class tree and "},{"code":"SwiduxObservable","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" conformance automatically. Hand-written conformance is still possible for advanced cases — see "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/SwiduxObservable"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"name":"Note","type":"aside","style":"note","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Explicit equality guards (","type":"text"},{"code":"if x != state.x { x = state.x }","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") are unnecessary. ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" already checks equality on ","type":"text"},{"code":"set","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". Unconditional assignment is safe.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"anchor":"Dispatch-Loop-Detection","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Dispatch Loop Detection"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PersistencePlugin"},{"text":" warns if ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"afterReduce"},{"type":"text","text":" is called more than 100 times per debounce interval. This usually means "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"send()"},{"text":" isn’t using the snapshot pattern, causing cascading re-renders that trigger re-dispatches.","type":"text"}]},{"anchor":"Reducer-Weight","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Reducer Weight"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Reducers run synchronously on the MainActor. Move O(n²) work into an "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/Effect","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" and dispatch the result back as an action."}]},{"anchor":"Effect-Threading","level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Effect Threading"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Effects run on Swift concurrency’s cooperative thread pool via ","type":"text"},{"code":"Task { @concurrent in }","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". Blocking calls (","type":"text"},{"code":"Process.waitUntilExit()","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"code":"DispatchSemaphore.wait()","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"code":"Thread.sleep()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") hold threads hostage. If enough block, the pool starves and the MainActor freezes.","type":"text"}]}],"style":"important","type":"aside","name":"Important"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Use async alternatives: ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"terminationHandler"},{"type":"text","text":" + continuation instead of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"waitUntilExit()"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task.sleep()"},{"type":"text","text":" instead of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Thread.sleep()"},{"type":"text","text":", async file I\/O instead of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Data(contentsOf:)"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/swidux\/architectureguide"]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Swidux\